#!/bin/bash
# cc-backlog — durable, append-only work-ledger for the desk mission (Program D).
#
# The ledger is EVIDENCE (one-artifact-one-role): every status change is an
# APPEND-ONLY record; the current status of an item is the FOLD of its record
# trail (last transition wins). Nothing is ever rewritten in place EXCEPT the
# `compact` verb, which may drop ONLY terminal (done) items that have aged past a
# threshold — open work is never touched. Malformed lines are reported, never
# silently dropped.
#
# Store: JSONL at ${CC_BACKLOG_FILE:-~/.claude/autonomy/backlog.jsonl}
# IDs are event-keyed (hash of project+title+source) → re-`add` is idempotent.
#
# Verbs:
#   add     --title T [--project P] [--dod-ref R] [--source S]   → echoes the id
#   list    [--open | --all | --blocked | --project P] [--json]  (default: --open, table)
#   claim   <id> --by <sid>
#   done    <id> --evidence <ref>
#   reopen  <id> [--by <sid>] [--force]
#   block   <id> --needs "<operator-only step>"                  → status "blocked"
#   unblock <id>                                                 → back to "open"
#   reap    [--dry-run]                                          → stale-claim maintenance sweep
#   compact [--older-than-days N]                                (default N=30)
#
# REOPEN GUARDS (added 2026-07-20 after a60d62a215f1 → 6488617 burned a peer session). `reopen`
# was the ONE transition with no status check at all: it would resurrect a terminal item and it
# would yank an item out from under a worker that was still running. Both put the item back at
# status "open", which is exactly cc-dispatch's fire predicate — so the next tick claimed and
# spawned on it. Two guards, both overridable by an explicit `--force`:
#
#   (1) TERMINAL — `reopen` of a `done` item is REFUSED (rc 4). Re-doing landed work is a pure
#       waste of a worker session. A genuine regression / premature `done` is re-opened
#       DELIBERATELY with `--force`, which is recorded in the trail as `force:true`.
#   (2) LIVE CLAIM — `reopen` of a `claimed` item whose claimer is PROVABLY live (the same
#       `claimer_live` oracle `reap` uses) and is not the caller is REFUSED (rc 4). This is the
#       mechanism that actually fired: the item was reopened 33 min into a live claim, and the
#       next dispatch tick claimed + spawned a second peer 15 s later.
#       Deliberately NOT refused: a claimer releasing its OWN claim (pass `--by <its sid>` — this
#       is cc-dispatch's spawn-fail rollback), and a claim whose owner is dead or unattributable
#       (that is `reap`'s dead-worker path — stranding the work is the worse failure).
#
# The DONE latch also survives the fold as `wasDone`, which cc-dispatch reads to keep completed
# work out of the wave even if something re-opened it by hand. `--force` is what clears it.
#
# BLOCKED-ON-OPERATOR: an item whose DoD needs an operator-only step (a key, a
# `launchctl bootout`, any human action) is `block`ed with the pending step in
# `--needs`. `blocked` folds distinctly from `open`, so cc-dispatch EXCLUDES it
# from the wave (no re-dispatch loop), while `list --open`/`--blocked` and the desk
# digest still SURFACE it + its `needs`. The operator `unblock`s once the step is
# done → back to `open` → dispatchable again.
#
# REAP (stale-claim maintenance — the dead-worker/thrash sweep): `block` above covers only
# OPERATOR-gated items and cc-reconcile heals only the SESSION registry — neither touches a
# CLAIM whose worker DIED. A `claim`ed item whose worker is gone (limit-killed, crashed, or
# self-closed without transitioning) stays `claimed` forever — cc-dispatch fires only
# status=="open", so the work STRANDS; a spawn-fail / land-conflict item THRASHES the wave
# (claim→reopen→claim…). `reap` folds the trail and, append-only + idempotent: (B, priority)
# BLOCKS an item with >= MAX_THRASH fast claim→reopen cycles (the spawn-fail / rebase-exit-5
# signature) so it leaves the wave for a human; (A) REOPENS a dead-worker claim idle past
# STALE_CLAIM_S whose claimer is not live — BOUNDED, so after MAX_ATTEMPTS dispatch claims a
# still-stale item is BLOCKED instead (never a slow-reopen loop). cc-reaper calls it each
# --reap sweep. All thresholds env-tunable (see the reap body).
#
# DEAD-WORKER EVIDENCE — the rule the whole sweep turns on: a dead verdict is keyed on PROCESS
# LIVENESS, never on elapsed claim time or attempt count. Elapsed time only GATES when to ask; it is
# never itself the answer. Under contention a healthy worker legitimately sits 5000s+ inside one
# full-suite gate, so a long claim is a signal of CONTENTION, not of death. Three oracles, and a
# reopen requires that one of them actually returned the verdict "not live":
#   1. CLAIMER  `kill -0` on a <host>-<pid> claim, else the cc-sessions registry (claimer_live).
#   2. WORKTREE  "not live" is asked of the CLAIMER, and a dispatched claim's `by` is cc-dispatch's
#      own spent pid — so before any dead verdict, reap re-verifies against the item's WORKTREE: a
#      live process tree there — named in a process's argv (a running gate / ship-land / queued
#      land-lock) OR occupying it as a process's CWD (the dispatched worker itself, whose launcher
#      carried the path and exited) — or the land lock held for its branch, is an OWNED WAIT: alive,
#      merely slow, and never called dead. A wait still present past OWNED_WAIT_MAX_S is a WEDGE:
#      blocked (never reopened — its worktree is occupied) and named as such. See the owned_wait body
#      for the full rationale and the incident it came from.
#   3. ABSTENTION  an oracle that produced NO ANSWER (registry absent, wedged, or unparseable) is a
#      NON-VERDICT, not a death: the item is KEPT and the abstention is printed. Bounded by
#      UNRESOLVED_MAX_S, past which it BLOCKS out to a human — it still never reopens, because an
#      unanswered probe is not proof of death at any age.
# Why the asymmetry is not merely tidy: `reopen` sets status=open, which is exactly cc-dispatch's
# fire predicate, so a false dead verdict SPAWNS A DUPLICATE PEER onto live work (a60d62a215f1 →
# 6488617). A missed dead worker only strands an item, visibly, until the next sweep.
set -uo pipefail

BACKLOG="${CC_BACKLOG_FILE:-$HOME/.claude/autonomy/backlog.jsonl}"

usage() {
  sed -n '2,/^set -uo/p' "$0" | sed 's/^# \{0,1\}//; /^set -uo/d'
}

now_iso()  { date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ; }

# cutoff_iso <days-ago> → an ISO-8601 UTC timestamp N days in the past (BSD + GNU).
cutoff_iso() {
  local d="$1"
  date -u -v-"${d}"d +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ 2>/dev/null \
    || date -u -d "-${d} days" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ 2>/dev/null
}

# mk_id <project> <title> <source> → 12-hex event key (US-separated, collision-safe).
mk_id() { printf '%s\037%s\037%s' "$1" "$2" "$3" | shasum -a 256 | cut -c1-12; }

ensure_file() {
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$BACKLOG")" 2>/dev/null || true
  [ -f "$BACKLOG" ] || : > "$BACKLOG"
}

# ── TSV field-collapse guard — docs/research/TSV_FIELD_COLLAPSE_2026-07-25.md ──────────────────
# Tab is IFS-*whitespace*, so `IFS=$'\t' read` collapses a RUN of tabs: ANY empty cell shifts every
# later field one position LEFT, silently and with a zero exit status. Here `.needs` is "" on every
# non-blocked item and `.project`/`.title` are attacker-free but not schema-guaranteed, so the row
# is padded at the `@tsv` EMITTER (the only durable fix — the read side cannot be repaired).
# `//` alone is NOT enough: it substitutes for null/false, never for a present-but-empty string.
# Board columns take a VISIBLE placeholder; `.needs` takes $'\037' because the reader needs the
# empty back (`[ -n "$needs" ]` is the blocked-row predicate) — see un-pad below.
CELL='def cell(ph): (if . == null then "" else . end) | tostring
      | gsub("[\\t\\r\\n]"; " ") | if . == "" then ph else . end; '
TSV_PAD=$'\037'

# Emit only well-formed records (must have id+event) to stdout; REPORT malformed
# lines to stderr (fail-loud). Never removes them from the file.
valid_records() {
  [ -f "$BACKLOG" ] || return 0
  # ONE jq pass, not one fork PER RECORD. jq startup (~6 ms) × a 1552-line APPEND-ONLY ledger was ~9 s
  # of every `cc-reaper --reap` tick — the second-largest cost in that sweep after the inbox guard —
  # and being append-only it could only ever grow. Contract is unchanged: well-formed lines VERBATIM
  # on stdout, malformed ones reported BY LINE NUMBER on stderr, nothing ever removed from the file.
  #
  # The line number is counted HERE rather than taken from jq's input_line_number, which counts
  # newlines CONSUMED — a final line with no trailing newline would share its predecessor's number.
  # One tag per input record (E/G/B) keeps the count exact and keeps a blank line "skipped", never
  # "malformed", exactly as the per-line loop did.
  local tagged n=0 bad=0 want
  while IFS= read -r tagged; do
    n=$((n + 1))
    case "$tagged" in
      G*) printf '%s\n' "${tagged#G}" ;;
      E*) : ;;                       # blank line — skipped silently, still counted (as before)
      *)  printf 'cc-backlog: malformed line %d skipped (still in file)\n' "$n" >&2; bad=$((bad + 1)) ;;
    esac
  done < <(jq -Rr 'if . == "" then "E"
                   elif (try (fromjson | (has("id") and has("event"))) catch false) then "G" + .
                   else "B" end' "$BACKLOG")
  # Fail LOUD on a short read. One jq covering the whole file means a jq failure would otherwise
  # report an EMPTY ledger — a silent "nothing to reap" instead of an error. Per-line forking could
  # not fail this way, so the check is what buys back that safety.
  # NB `grep -c ''` prints 0 and EXITS 1 on an empty file, so `|| echo 0` would append a second 0 and
  # make `want` the two-word "0 0" — which fails the -eq and cries INCOMPLETE on every empty ledger.
  want="$(grep -c '' "$BACKLOG" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  case "$want" in ''|*[!0-9]*) want=0 ;; esac
  [ "$n" -eq "$want" ] 2>/dev/null || \
    printf 'cc-backlog: record scan INCOMPLETE — %s of %s line(s) read; ledger NOT fully reported\n' "$n" "$want" >&2
  [ "$bad" -eq 0 ] || printf 'cc-backlog: %d malformed line(s) reported above\n' "$bad" >&2
  return 0
}

# Fold the trail → a JSON ARRAY of one object per id (latest status + carried meta).
fold() {
  valid_records | jq -s '
    reduce .[] as $r ({};
      .[$r.id] = ((.[$r.id] // {}) as $p | {
        id:       $r.id,
        project:  ($r.project  // $p.project  // ""),
        title:    ($r.title    // $p.title    // ""),
        dodRef:   ($r.dodRef   // $p.dodRef   // ""),
        source:   ($r.source   // $p.source   // ""),
        by:       ($r.by       // $p.by       // ""),
        evidence: ($r.evidence // $p.evidence // ""),
        needs:    ($r.needs    // $p.needs    // ""),
        status: (
          if   $r.event == "done"    then "done"
          elif $r.event == "block"   then "blocked"
          elif $r.event == "unblock" then "open"
          elif $r.event == "claim"   then "claimed"
          elif $r.event == "reopen"  then "open"
          else ($p.status // "open") end),
        # wasDone — the DONE LATCH (not the same thing as status=="done"). It sticks once the
        # item has EVER been completed and is cleared ONLY by a deliberate `reopen --force`, so
        # a re-opened-by-hand item still reads "this was already finished once". cc-dispatch
        # fires on `status=="open" and wasDone != true`, which is what keeps landed work out of
        # the wave after any reopen path (guarded, forced, or a hand-appended JSONL line).
        wasDone: (
          if   $r.event == "done"                            then true
          elif ($r.event == "reopen" and ($r.force // false)) then false
          else ($p.wasDone // false) end),
        lastTs:   ($r.ts // $p.lastTs // "")
      })
    ) | [ .[] ]'
}

# item_json <id> → the folded object for <id> (compact JSON), or "" when the id is unknown.
# stderr is dropped: callers here are GUARDS, and a malformed-line report belongs to the verb
# the user actually ran (list/reap), not to a status peek inside a transition.
item_json() { fold 2>/dev/null | jq -c --arg i "$1" 'map(select(.id == $i)) | first // empty' 2>/dev/null; }

has_id() {
  [ -f "$BACKLOG" ] || return 1
  valid_records 2>/dev/null | jq -e -s --arg id "$1" 'any(.[]; .id == $id)' >/dev/null 2>&1
}

cmd_add() {
  local project="" title="" dod="" source=""
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    local key="$1"; shift
    case "$key" in
      --project) project="${1:-}"; [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift ;;
      --title)   title="${1:-}";   [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift ;;
      --dod-ref) dod="${1:-}";     [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift ;;
      --source)  source="${1:-}";  [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift ;;
      *) printf 'cc-backlog add: unknown arg %s\n' "$key" >&2; return 2 ;;
    esac
  done
  [ -n "$title" ] || { echo "cc-backlog add: --title is required" >&2; return 2; }
  [ -n "$project" ] || project="$(pwd)"
  ensure_file
  local id; id=$(mk_id "$project" "$title" "$source")
  if has_id "$id"; then                            # idempotent: no duplicate add
    # DONE-GUARD (add side): the event key (project+title+source) resolves to an item that is
    # already TERMINAL — this `add` is a re-file of completed work. The id is still echoed with
    # rc 0 (the idempotency contract `cc-discover` and every other caller depends on) and the
    # item is deliberately NOT re-opened, but the collision is announced LOUD so a stale re-file
    # can never read as fresh work.
    local ex; ex="$(item_json "$id")"
    if [ "$(printf '%s' "$ex" | jq -r '.status // ""' 2>/dev/null)" = "done" ]; then
      { printf 'cc-backlog add: WARNING — this event-key is already DONE; NOT re-opened.\n'
        printf '  id %s · done %s\n' "$id" "$(printf '%s' "$ex" | jq -r '.lastTs // "?"')"
        printf '  evidence: %s\n' "$(printf '%s' "$ex" | jq -r 'if (.evidence // "") == "" then "<none>" else .evidence end')"
        printf '  Genuinely new work needs a distinct title/source (the id is a hash of\n'
        printf '  project+title+source). If THIS work regressed:  cc-backlog reopen %s --force\n' "$id"
      } >&2
    fi
    echo "$id"; return 0
  fi
  jq -nc --arg id "$id" --arg ts "$(now_iso)" --arg project "$project" \
     --arg title "$title" --arg dod "$dod" --arg source "$source" \
     '{id:$id, ts:$ts, event:"add", project:$project, title:$title, dodRef:$dod, source:$source}' \
     >> "$BACKLOG"
  echo "$id"
}

cmd_transition() {
  local event="$1"; shift
  local id="${1:-}"; [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift
  local by="" evidence="" needs="" force=0
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    local key="$1"; shift
    case "$key" in
      --by)       by="${1:-}";       [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift ;;
      --evidence) evidence="${1:-}"; [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift ;;
      --needs)    needs="${1:-}";    [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift ;;
      --force)    force=1 ;;
      *) printf 'cc-backlog %s: unknown arg %s\n' "$event" "$key" >&2; return 2 ;;
    esac
  done
  [ -n "$id" ] || { printf 'cc-backlog %s: <id> is required\n' "$event" >&2; return 2; }
  # `block` MUST carry the pending operator step — that IS the desk-surfaced payload.
  if [ "$event" = block ] && [ -z "$needs" ]; then
    printf 'cc-backlog block: --needs "<operator-only step>" is required\n' >&2; return 2
  fi
  # --force is the reopen-guard override and nothing else — never silently ignored elsewhere.
  if [ "$force" -eq 1 ] && [ "$event" != reopen ]; then
    printf 'cc-backlog %s: --force applies only to reopen\n' "$event" >&2; return 2
  fi
  ensure_file
  has_id "$id" || { printf 'cc-backlog %s: unknown id %s\n' "$event" "$id" >&2; return 3; }
  # REOPEN GUARDS (see the header block) — refuse a resurrection of terminal work and a yank of a
  # live claim. Both are rc 4 and both name the exact `--force` escape in the refusal.
  if [ "$event" = reopen ] && [ "$force" -eq 0 ]; then
    local cur cstatus cby cev cts
    cur="$(item_json "$id")"
    cstatus="$(printf '%s' "$cur" | jq -r '.status   // ""' 2>/dev/null)"
    cby="$(    printf '%s' "$cur" | jq -r '.by       // ""' 2>/dev/null)"
    if [ "$cstatus" = "done" ]; then
      cev="$(printf '%s' "$cur" | jq -r 'if (.evidence // "") == "" then "<none>" else .evidence end' 2>/dev/null)"
      cts="$(printf '%s' "$cur" | jq -r '.lastTs // "?"' 2>/dev/null)"
      { printf 'cc-backlog reopen: REFUSED — %s is TERMINAL (done %s)\n' "$id" "$cts"
        printf '  evidence: %s\n' "$cev"
        printf '  Re-opening completed work puts it back in the dispatch wave and burns a worker\n'
        printf '  session re-doing what already landed (incident 2026-07-20, a60d62a215f1).\n'
        printf '  If the work genuinely regressed, or the completion was premature, say so explicitly:\n'
        printf '      cc-backlog reopen %s --force\n' "$id"
      } >&2
      return 4
    fi
    if [ "$cstatus" = claimed ] && [ -n "$cby" ] && [ "$cby" != "$by" ] && claimer_live "$cby"; then
      { printf 'cc-backlog reopen: REFUSED — %s is claimed by %s, which is still LIVE\n' "$id" "$cby"
        printf '  A live claim returned to "open" is re-dispatchable: the next cc-dispatch tick\n'
        printf '  claims it and spawns a SECOND worker onto work already in progress. That is the\n'
        printf '  2026-07-20 incident exactly (reopened 33 min into a live claim, re-claimed 15 s later).\n'
        printf '  The claimer releases its own claim with:  cc-backlog reopen %s --by %s\n' "$id" "$cby"
        printf '  A DEAD claimer needs no force — cc-backlog reap reopens it once it goes stale.\n'
        printf '  To override deliberately:  cc-backlog reopen %s --force\n' "$id"
      } >&2
      return 4
    fi
  fi
  jq -nc --arg id "$id" --arg ts "$(now_iso)" --arg event "$event" \
     --arg by "$by" --arg evidence "$evidence" --arg needs "$needs" --argjson force "$force" \
     '{id:$id, ts:$ts, event:$event}
      + (if $by != ""       then {by:$by}             else {} end)
      + (if $evidence != "" then {evidence:$evidence} else {} end)
      + (if $needs != ""    then {needs:$needs}       else {} end)
      + (if $force == 1     then {force:true}         else {} end)' \
     >> "$BACKLOG"
  echo "$id"
}

cmd_list() {
  local mode="open" projfilter="" fmt="table"
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    local key="$1"; shift
    case "$key" in
      --open)    mode="open" ;;
      --all)     mode="all" ;;
      --blocked) mode="blocked" ;;
      --project) projfilter="${1:-}"; [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift ;;
      --json) fmt="json" ;;
      *) printf 'cc-backlog list: unknown arg %s\n' "$key" >&2; return 2 ;;
    esac
  done
  # mode → status predicate (`keep`, shared by json + table): all=every item ·
  # blocked=only blocked · open(default)=every non-done item (so `blocked` items STILL
  # surface in the desk's --open view, but cc-dispatch's exact status=="open" filter
  # excludes them). `needs` (the pending operator step) is carried in both branches.
  # --json: the SAME filtered fold as the table, as a compact JSON array (one object per
  # item, full carried meta incl. `needs`) for machine consumers (cc-dispatch, the desk
  # digest). The default (table) branch is byte-unchanged for non-blocked rows.
  if [ "$fmt" = json ]; then
    fold | jq -c --arg mode "$mode" --arg proj "$projfilter" '
      def keep: if $mode=="all" then true elif $mode=="blocked" then .status=="blocked" else .status!="done" end;
      sort_by(.project, .title)
      | map( select(keep)
           | select($proj == "" or .project == $proj) )'
    return 0
  fi
  fold | jq -r --arg mode "$mode" --arg proj "$projfilter" --arg pad "$TSV_PAD" "$CELL"'
    def keep: if $mode=="all" then true elif $mode=="blocked" then .status=="blocked" else .status!="done" end;
    sort_by(.project, .title) | .[]
    | select(keep)
    | select($proj == "" or .project == $proj)
    | [ (.status | cell("?")), (.id | cell("?")), (.project | cell("?")),
        (.title | cell("?")), (.needs | cell($pad)) ] | @tsv
  ' | while IFS=$'\t' read -r st id proj title needs; do
      [ "$needs" = "$TSV_PAD" ] && needs=""      # un-pad: "" is the not-blocked signal
      if [ "$st" = blocked ] && [ -n "$needs" ]; then
        printf '%-8s | %s | %-28s | %s  ⟵ needs: %s\n' "$st" "$id" "$proj" "$title" "$needs"
      else
        printf '%-8s | %s | %-28s | %s\n' "$st" "$id" "$proj" "$title"
      fi
    done
}

cmd_compact() {
  local days="30"
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    local key="$1"; shift
    case "$key" in
      --older-than-days) days="${1:-30}"; [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift ;;
      *) printf 'cc-backlog compact: unknown arg %s\n' "$key" >&2; return 2 ;;
    esac
  done
  ensure_file
  local cutoff; cutoff=$(cutoff_iso "$days")
  [ -n "$cutoff" ] || { echo "cc-backlog compact: could not compute cutoff" >&2; return 2; }
  # Drop set: terminal (done) items whose last event is older than the cutoff.
  local drop; drop=$(fold | jq -r --arg c "$cutoff" \
    '.[] | select(.status == "done" and .lastTs != "" and .lastTs < $c) | .id')
  if [ -z "$drop" ]; then
    printf 'cc-backlog compact: nothing aged out (cutoff %s)\n' "$cutoff" >&2
    return 0
  fi
  local tmp n=0 kept=0 dropped=0 malformed=0 line lid
  tmp=$(mktemp)
  while IFS= read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]; do
    n=$((n + 1)); [ -z "$line" ] && continue
    lid=$(printf '%s' "$line" | jq -r 'if (has("id") and has("event")) then .id else "__BAD__" end' 2>/dev/null || echo "__BAD__")
    if [ "$lid" = "__BAD__" ]; then
      printf '%s\n' "$line" >> "$tmp"; malformed=$((malformed + 1))   # preserve, never drop
      printf 'cc-backlog compact: preserved malformed line %d\n' "$n" >&2
      continue
    fi
    if printf '%s\n' "$drop" | grep -qxF -- "$lid"; then
      dropped=$((dropped + 1)); continue
    fi
    printf '%s\n' "$line" >> "$tmp"; kept=$((kept + 1))
  done < "$BACKLOG"
  mv "$tmp" "$BACKLOG"
  printf 'cc-backlog compact: kept %d, dropped %d aged-terminal, preserved %d malformed\n' \
    "$kept" "$dropped" "$malformed" >&2
}

# _resolve_sessions_bin → path to cc-sessions (the live-registry liveness oracle), or "" (rc 0).
# Env override wins (tests), then sibling, then ~/.claude/bin, then PATH (launchd bare-PATH safe).
_resolve_sessions_bin() {
  if [ -n "${CC_BACKLOG_SESSIONS_BIN:-}" ]; then printf '%s' "$CC_BACKLOG_SESSIONS_BIN"; return 0; fi
  local c
  for c in "$(dirname "$0")/cc-sessions" "$HOME/.claude/bin/cc-sessions" "$(command -v cc-sessions 2>/dev/null || true)"; do
    [ -n "$c" ] && [ -x "$c" ] && { printf '%s' "$c"; return 0; }
  done
  printf ''
}

# claimer_live <by> — is the claim's owner still live? A `<host>-<pid>` claimer (cc-dispatch and
# most manual claims) is authoritatively checked with `kill -0` (same user across accounts); a
# session-id / UUID / name claimer against the live cc-sessions registry. Our own reap marker is
# never "live".
#
# THREE outcomes, not two — the distinction is the whole point (memory: gate-never-ran-vs-gate-red,
# claimed-outcome-vs-checked-outcome — one exit code for two outcomes forces every caller to lie):
#   rc 0  LIVE       proven: `kill -0` succeeded, or the registry ANSWERED and lists this claimer.
#   rc 1  NOT-LIVE   proven: `kill -0` failed on a well-formed <host>-<pid>, or the registry ANSWERED
#                    and does not list the claimer, or there is no owner to check at all (empty `by`,
#                    our own reap marker). A real verdict — the dead-worker path may act on it.
#   rc 2  UNRESOLVED the PROBE produced no answer: no registry binary, the call timed out (a wedged
#                    it2), empty output, or unparseable output. A NON-VERDICT — the absence of an
#                    answer, not the answer "dead". Callers must ABSTAIN on this, never convict: our
#                    own timeout must not forge the kill evidence.
#
# rc 2 used to be folded into rc 1 ("an unresolved oracle reads NOT-live — stranding the work is the
# worse failure"). That trade was wrong in the direction it was made: `reopen` sets status=open,
# which is cc-dispatch's fire predicate, so convicting on a non-verdict does not merely strand work —
# it SPAWNS A SECOND WORKER onto work that may well be live. Stranding is visible, bounded and
# recoverable (the abstention ceiling below blocks it out to a human); double-dispatch is neither.
claimer_live() {
  local by="$1"
  [ -n "$by" ] && [ "$by" != cc-backlog-reap ] || return 1
  local host; host="$(hostname -s 2>/dev/null || hostname 2>/dev/null || echo localhost)"
  local pid="${by##*-}"
  if [ "$by" = "$host-$pid" ] && [[ "$pid" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
    if kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null; then return 0; fi
    return 1
  fi
  local sb; sb="$(_resolve_sessions_bin)"
  [ -n "$sb" ] || return 2          # no registry to ask ⇒ UNRESOLVED, not "dead"
  # TIME-CAP the registry fork. cc-sessions resolves pane liveness through `it2 session list --json`
  # (cc-sessions:57) with no timeout of its own, and a WEDGED it2 API hangs that call indefinitely —
  # measured 2026-07-26 during a machine-wide wedge: `timeout 60 cc-sessions --json` → rc 124, and a
  # bare `it2 session list --json` → rc 124 with no output. Unwrapped, one session-shaped claimer
  # would hang `reap` forever and take the whole `cc-reaper --reap` sweep down with it — and the
  # owned-wait re-verify below would never be REACHED, leaving this fix inert by construction in
  # exactly the incident where correct stall verdicts matter most (memory:
  # feature-durability-mechanism-not-memory). A cap composes cleanly with the rc contract above: a
  # timed-out probe is UNRESOLVED (rc 2, a non-verdict) — it is NOT evidence of death, precisely
  # because the timeout is OURS. Our own `timeout` must never be allowed to forge the kill evidence
  # (memory: gate-never-ran-vs-gate-red).
  local sessions_json
  if command -v timeout >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    sessions_json="$(timeout "${CC_BACKLOG_ORACLE_TIMEOUT_S:-10}" "$sb" --json 2>/dev/null || true)"
  else
    sessions_json="$("$sb" --json 2>/dev/null || true)"
  fi
  [ -n "$sessions_json" ] || return 2          # no output (timeout / crash) ⇒ UNRESOLVED
  # jq's own rc carries the third state for free: 0 = listed (LIVE), 1 = answered-and-absent (a real
  # NOT-LIVE verdict), anything else = jq could not parse/evaluate the registry ⇒ UNRESOLVED.
  printf '%s' "$sessions_json" \
    | jq -e --arg b "$by" 'any(.[]?; (.paneUUID==$b) or (.name==$b) or (.sessionId==$b))' >/dev/null 2>&1
  local jrc=$?
  case "$jrc" in 0) return 0 ;; 1) return 1 ;; *) return 2 ;; esac
}

# item_worktree <id> → the dispatch worktree an item's worker runs in, by the SAME convention
# cc-wave-plan builds its --cwd from ($WTROOT/wt-<id>, cc-wave-plan:45). Env seam mirrors that name.
item_worktree() { printf '%s/wt-%s' "${CC_BACKLOG_WT_ROOT:-$HOME/Development/.worktrees}" "$1"; }

# procs_cwd_under <dir> → pids of live processes whose CWD is at or below <dir>, one per line.
# macOS has no /proc, so a process's cwd is only readable through lsof(8) — a SYSTEM binary
# (/usr/sbin/lsof), present under the bare PATH a launchd sweep runs with. `-d cwd -F pn` is the terse
# form: a `p<pid>` line, then the `n<path>` for that process's cwd. The PREFIX match lives in awk
# rather than in an lsof path argument because passing the path makes lsof match it EXACTLY — which
# misses a worker that has cd'd into a subdirectory (measured: such a process was invisible to the
# path-argument form and visible to this one, at an identical 0.22s).
#
# BOUNDED: lsof can block indefinitely on an unresponsive mount, and this runs inside the
# `cc-reaper --reap` sweep — an unbounded probe would hang that sweep exactly as the wedged it2
# registry call did (see claimer_live). A timed-out probe yields NO pids, so the signal abstains and
# the next one is consulted: a bound costs a slower path, never a false ALIVE verdict.
#
# PATH FORM: lsof reports a cwd PHYSICALLY RESOLVED — a process sitting in /var/… is reported under
# /private/var/… (measured 2026-07-26). A prefix match against the RAW path therefore sees NOTHING
# whenever any component of the worktree root is a symlink: the probe reads "no processes", the
# oracle abstains, and reap convicts a live worker — the exact false-dead this signal exists to
# remove, reintroduced one level down. Match BOTH the raw path and its `pwd -P` resolution; they are
# identical when nothing is symlinked, so this only ever ADDS a match and can never drop one.
#
# Seam: CC_BACKLOG_LSOF_BIN — UNSET ⇒ resolve one. SET, including set to EMPTY ⇒ honored verbatim, so
# `CC_BACKLOG_LSOF_BIN=` genuinely disables S1b. `${VAR:-}` cannot tell unset from set-empty, and a
# seam that cannot turn a thing OFF is not a seam (memory: claimed-outcome-vs-checked-outcome).
procs_cwd_under() {
  local dir="$1" bin out pdir
  pdir="$(cd "$dir" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P 2>/dev/null)" || pdir=""
  if [ -n "${CC_BACKLOG_LSOF_BIN+set}" ]; then
    bin="$CC_BACKLOG_LSOF_BIN"
  else
    bin=/usr/sbin/lsof
    [ -x "$bin" ] || bin="$(command -v lsof 2>/dev/null || true)"
  fi
  [ -n "$bin" ] && [ -x "$bin" ] || return 0
  if command -v timeout >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    out="$(timeout "${CC_BACKLOG_ORACLE_TIMEOUT_S:-10}" "$bin" -a -d cwd -w -F pn 2>/dev/null || true)"
  else
    out="$("$bin" -a -d cwd -w -F pn 2>/dev/null || true)"
  fi
  [ -n "$out" ] || return 0
  printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -v d="$dir" -v pd="$pdir" '
    function under(n, base) { return base != "" && (n == base || index(n, base "/") == 1) }
    /^p/ { p = substr($0, 2); next }
    /^n/ { n = substr($0, 2); if (under(n, d) || under(n, pd)) print p }' | sort -u
}

# owned_wait <id> — is the item's WORKER in an OWNED WAIT (alive, merely slow) rather than DEAD?
# Echoes a short reason and returns 0 when alive; returns 1 (silent) when there is no evidence.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (2026-07-26, backlog 2d36e63d16a2). `claimer_live` alone cannot see a dispatched
# worker: cc-dispatch claims with `--by $SID` where SID is its OWN `$$` (cc-dispatch:58), then spawns
# the session and EXITS. Its pid is gone within seconds while the worker it spawned runs for hours —
# so past STALE_CLAIM_S, `claimer_live` is false BY CONSTRUCTION for every dispatched item and Rule A
# degrades to a pure idle-time verdict. Three items with live gates were blocked "dead-worker stall
# … not auto-completable" that way (02ba4e52389a / 761a546f939c / 6cab0ab3cb2f), which is not benign:
# a false dead verdict pulls the item OFF the auto-drive path and feeds dispatcher re-spawn. This is
# the same class as memory desk-wave-monitor-lead-idle-is-owned-wait (a lead's JSONL freeze during a
# wave is waiting on teammates, not a stall) and desk-verify-fresh-before-mutating-backlog (the claim
# `by` is the DISPATCHER, not the worker — check WORKTREE liveness). Dead-evidence must read whether
# a wait is OWNED, never merely how long it has been quiet.
#
# The claim's `by` cannot be fixed at the source instead: cc-dispatch has no worker identity to claim
# with (the spawned session's sid does not exist until it registers), so liveness has to be keyed on
# the worktree — the one durable handle that outlives the dispatcher.
#
# Signals (any ⇒ owned):
#   S1  a live process whose command line names the item's worktree — the full-suite gate
#       (bats-exec-suite/-file/-test), ship-land, a queued land-lock, a task-output wait loop.
#   S1b a live process whose CWD is at or below the worktree. S1 alone CANNOT see the worker itself
#       (measured 2026-07-26 against a live dispatched session, backlog b1b7a425e169): cc-wave-plan
#       fires `handoff-fire.sh --cwd $WTROOT/wt-<id>` (cc-wave-plan:287), so the worktree path lives
#       in the LAUNCHER's argv — and the launcher exits once the pane is up. The surviving `claude`
#       inherits only the cwd, so `pgrep -f "$wt"` returned 0 while SIX live processes (the session,
#       its shells, its crash watchdog, caffeinate) sat in that worktree. That is the SAME
#       one-level-down defect S1 was written to fix: the handle that names the worktree belongs to a
#       process that is already spent. Keying on cwd reads the worker directly.
#       NOT a bare-id `pgrep -f <id>`: a session's argv embeds its task brief, so the id alone also
#       matches the desk merely DISCUSSING the item (memory: effect-read-predicate-red-proof — a
#       positive is not execution). CWD is occupancy, not mention.
#       Matched by PREFIX, not equality: a worker that has cd'd into a subdirectory is still working
#       (exact-path lsof missed such a process in the same measurement).
#   S2  the machine-wide land lock held for THIS item's branch by a live holder. Distinct from S1,
#       not redundant: a land re-run from the shared checkout (or any other cwd) on branch wt-<id>
#       names no worktree path in its argv, yet is the most owned wait there is.
#
# Deliberately NOT filtered: reap's own process tree — by argv (S1) or by cwd (S1b). A process inside
# wt-<id> is evidence that work in wt-<id> is live — including a worker session that ran
# `cc-backlog reap` itself. The real hazard is an ORPHANED wait pinning an item forever, and the
# caller's OWNED_WAIT_MAX_S ceiling is what bounds that (a 6h "gate" is pathological, not owned). In
# production the reaper resolves cc-backlog through ~/.claude/bin → the shared checkout, so neither
# its argv nor its cwd carries a wt-<id> path at all.
owned_wait() {
  local id="$1" wt; wt="$(item_worktree "$id")"
  [ -d "$wt" ] || return 1

  # S1 — live process tree in the worktree. `pgrep -f` excludes itself; one fork, only ever reached
  # for a claim already past the stale gate, so this costs nothing on a healthy ledger.
  local procs; procs="$(pgrep -f "$wt" 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')"
  if [ -n "$procs" ] && [ "$procs" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then
    printf 'live process tree in %s (%s proc)' "$wt" "$procs"; return 0
  fi

  # S1b — live process whose CWD is at or below the worktree (see the header: this is the signal
  # that actually sees a dispatched worker; S1 sees only the spent launcher's argv).
  local cwdprocs; cwdprocs="$(procs_cwd_under "$wt" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')"
  if [ -n "$cwdprocs" ] && [ "$cwdprocs" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then
    printf 'live process cwd in %s (%s proc)' "$wt" "$cwdprocs"; return 0
  fi

  # S2 — land lock held for this item's branch. The lock dir is a hash of the repo's common git dir,
  # so ask land-lock.sh itself (`--print-lock-dir` is its documented pure read) from inside the
  # worktree rather than duplicating the hash. Holder liveness uses the pid+lstart rule land-lock
  # uses, because a dead holder's pid can be RECYCLED (memory: periodic-job-self-overlap).
  local ll="$wt/scripts/land-lock.sh"
  [ -x "$ll" ] || return 1
  local lockdir; lockdir="$(cd "$wt" && "$ll" --print-lock-dir 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  [ -n "$lockdir" ] || return 1
  local lock="$lockdir/lock.d"
  [ "$(cat "$lock/branch" 2>/dev/null)" = "wt-$id" ] || return 1
  local holder; holder="$(cat "$lock/pid" 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$holder" ] && kill -0 "$holder" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  local rec_lstart cur_lstart
  rec_lstart="$(cat "$lock/lstart" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  cur_lstart="$(ps -o lstart= -p "$holder" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  [ -z "$rec_lstart" ] || [ "$rec_lstart" = "$cur_lstart" ] || return 1
  printf 'land lock held for wt-%s by live pid %s' "$id" "$holder"
}

# cmd_reap — the stale-claim maintenance sweep (see the REAP header block). Append-only, idempotent,
# and safe to run every cc-reaper sweep. Terminal (done) and already-blocked items are never touched.
# --dry-run classifies + prints WOULD-* and writes NOTHING.
#
# Thresholds (all env-tunable; defaults calibrated from 75 real claim→done items — p50 33min / p90
# 55min, so a live worker under p90 is never double-dispatched by the 90-min stale gate):
#   CC_BACKLOG_STALE_CLAIM_S    (5400)  claimed idle past this ⇒ dead-worker candidate → reopen
#   CC_BACKLOG_MAX_THRASH       (2)     >= this many fast claim→reopen cycles ⇒ thrash → block
#   CC_BACKLOG_MAX_ATTEMPTS     (3)     >= this many total dispatch claims on a still-stale item ⇒ block
#   CC_BACKLOG_THRASH_WINDOW_S  (90)    a claim→reopen within this gap counts as one fast-fail cycle
#   CC_BACKLOG_OWNED_WAIT_MAX_S (21600) ceiling past which even a live worktree wait stops absolving
#                                       — an orphaned watcher must not pin an item forever (6h; a
#                                       full ~1630-test suite is 25-40min nominal, hours under the
#                                       load-20 contention that produced the false verdicts)
#   CC_BACKLOG_UNRESOLVED_MAX_S (=OWNED_WAIT_MAX_S) ceiling past which an UNRESOLVED claimer (rc 2 —
#                                       registry absent/wedged) stops absolving and is BLOCKED out to
#                                       a human. Abstention must be bounded for the same reason the
#                                       owned wait is: a permanently-broken registry would otherwise
#                                       pin every session-shaped claim as "undecidable" forever. Past
#                                       the ceiling the item still never REOPENS — an unanswered
#                                       probe is not proof of death at any age.
#   CC_BACKLOG_WT_ROOT          (~/Development/.worktrees)  dispatch worktree parent (see owned_wait)
#   CC_BACKLOG_NOW              (now)   epoch "now" seam (deterministic age/window math in tests)
cmd_reap() {
  local dry=0
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      --dry-run) dry=1 ;;
      *) printf 'cc-backlog reap: unknown arg %s\n' "$1" >&2; return 2 ;;
    esac
    shift
  done
  ensure_file
  local stale="${CC_BACKLOG_STALE_CLAIM_S:-5400}"
  local maxthrash="${CC_BACKLOG_MAX_THRASH:-2}"
  local maxattempts="${CC_BACKLOG_MAX_ATTEMPTS:-3}"
  local window="${CC_BACKLOG_THRASH_WINDOW_S:-90}"
  local ownedmax="${CC_BACKLOG_OWNED_WAIT_MAX_S:-21600}"
  local unresolvedmax="${CC_BACKLOG_UNRESOLVED_MAX_S:-$ownedmax}"
  local now="${CC_BACKLOG_NOW:-$(date +%s)}"

  # Per non-terminal id → TSV [id, status, claimBy, claimAgeS, totalClaims, fastFailCycles].
  # status = last-event fold; claimBy/claimTs from the LAST claim (not our reap-reopen); fastFail =
  # adjacent claim→reopen pairs within $window (the intervening-done/block case can't be adjacent).
  local analysis
  analysis="$(valid_records | jq -s -r --argjson now "$now" --argjson window "$window" '
    def st($e): if $e=="done" then "done" elif $e=="block" then "blocked"
      elif $e=="unblock" then "open" elif $e=="claim" then "claimed"
      elif $e=="reopen" then "open" else "open" end;
    group_by(.id)
    | map(
        (sort_by(.ts)) as $g
        # Thrash window resets at the LATEST block/unblock record: fastFail counts only claim→reopen
        # pairs occurring AFTER it. Without this, a deliberate `unblock` (status→open) is re-blocked by
        # the very next reap on pre-unblock history that still folds ≥ MAX_THRASH — the dispatcher-
        # starvation bug (unblock never survives cc-reaper). A genuinely NEW post-unblock thrash still
        # blocks (the counter only ever narrows here, never widens what the blocker acts on). Default
        # cut = -1 (no block/unblock in trail) preserves the original whole-trail fold.
        | ([ range(0; ($g|length)) as $j
             | select($g[$j].event=="unblock" or $g[$j].event=="block") | $j ] | last // -1) as $cut
        | ($g[-1]) as $last
        | { id: $last.id,
            status: st($last.event),
            claimBy: ([$g[] | select(.event=="claim")] | last | .by // ""),
            claimTs: ([$g[] | select(.event=="claim")] | last | .ts // ""),
            totalClaims: ([$g[] | select(.event=="claim")] | length),
            fastFail: ([ range(0; ($g|length)-1) as $i
                | select($i > $cut
                         and $g[$i].event=="claim" and $g[$i+1].event=="reopen"
                         and (($g[$i+1].ts|fromdateiso8601) - ($g[$i].ts|fromdateiso8601)) <= $window) ]
              | length) }
        | .claimAgeS = (if .claimTs=="" then -1 else ($now - (.claimTs|fromdateiso8601)) end)
      )
    | .[]
    | select(.status != "done" and .status != "blocked")
    | [.id, .status, .claimBy, (.claimAgeS|floor|tostring), (.totalClaims|tostring), (.fastFail|tostring)]
    | join("\u001f")
  ')"

  # Split on US (\037), NOT tab: an open item with no claim has an EMPTY claimBy field, and bash
  # `read` COALESCES consecutive whitespace-IFS chars (tab included) — two adjacent tabs would drop
  # the empty field and shift every later column left (fast → empty → "integer expression" error).
  # A non-whitespace IFS preserves empty fields positionally.
  local reopened=0 blocked=0 scanned=0 id status by ageS claims fast clrc=0
  while IFS=$'\037' read -r id status by ageS claims fast; do
    [ -n "$id" ] || continue
    scanned=$((scanned + 1))
    # Rule B (priority): persistent thrash → block (out of the wave, surfaced to the desk).
    if [ "$fast" -ge "$maxthrash" ]; then
      local bmsg="persistent thrash — ${fast} fast claim→reopen cycle(s) (spawn-fail / land-conflict rebase-exit-5); the worker cannot land. Investigate the root cause, then \`cc-backlog unblock ${id}\`."
      if [ "$dry" = 1 ]; then
        printf 'cc-backlog reap: WOULD-BLOCK %s [thrash x%s]\n' "$id" "$fast"
      elif cmd_transition block "$id" --needs "$bmsg" --by cc-backlog-reap >/dev/null; then
        blocked=$((blocked + 1)); printf 'cc-backlog reap: BLOCK %s [thrash x%s]\n' "$id" "$fast"
      fi
      continue
    fi
    # Rule A: dead-worker stale claim → reopen (bounded → block after MAX_ATTEMPTS).
    if [ "$status" = claimed ] && [ "$ageS" -gt "$stale" ]; then
      # rc is THREE-valued (see claimer_live): 0 LIVE, 1 proven NOT-LIVE, 2 UNRESOLVED (non-verdict).
      # Captured, not tested inline, because only rc 1 may ever reach the reopen path.
      claimer_live "$by"; clrc=$?
      if [ "$clrc" -eq 0 ]; then
        [ "$dry" = 1 ] && printf 'cc-backlog reap: keep %s [claimed %ss — claimer %s LIVE]\n' "$id" "$ageS" "$by"
        continue
      fi
      # RE-VERIFY before any dead verdict: the claim's `by` is the dispatcher's spent pid, so
      # claimer_live cannot see the worker (see owned_wait). Ask the WORKTREE whether the wait is
      # OWNED. Printed in BOTH modes on purpose — a silent absolve is indistinguishable from an
      # inert oracle (memory: feature-durability-mechanism-not-memory).
      local owned; owned="$(owned_wait "$id" || true)"
      if [ -n "$owned" ] && [ "$ageS" -le "$ownedmax" ]; then
        printf 'cc-backlog reap: KEEP %s [owned wait — %s; claimed %ss]\n' "$id" "$owned" "$ageS"
        continue
      fi
      # ABSTAIN on a non-verdict. Every oracle has now spoken and none of them said "dead": the
      # claimer probe gave NO ANSWER (rc 2) and the worktree shows nothing. That is ignorance, not
      # evidence — and the transition it would otherwise feed is `reopen`, i.e. cc-dispatch's fire
      # predicate. Printed in BOTH modes: a silent abstain is indistinguishable from an inert oracle
      # (memory: feature-durability-mechanism-not-memory).
      if [ -z "$owned" ] && [ "$clrc" -eq 2 ] && [ "$ageS" -le "$unresolvedmax" ]; then
        printf 'cc-backlog reap: KEEP %s [claimer UNRESOLVED — %s; no answer is not proof of death, abstaining; claimed %ss]\n' \
          "$id" "${by:-<none>}" "$ageS"
        continue
      fi
      # A WEDGED owned wait (live worktree, but past the ceiling) BLOCKS regardless of the attempt
      # count — it never reopens. Reopening would fire a second worker into a worktree the first one
      # still occupies, which is the very double-dispatch this oracle exists to prevent; and the
      # verdict is a wedge to diagnose, not a death to re-run. An UNRESOLVED claimer past its own
      # ceiling blocks for the mirror-image reason: still not proof of death, so still never reopened
      # — but the abstention itself has to end somewhere, and it ends at a human.
      local why="stall, $claims attempts"
      if [ "$claims" -ge "$maxattempts" ] || [ -n "$owned" ] || [ "$clrc" -eq 2 ]; then
        local smsg="dead-worker stall after ${claims} dispatch attempt(s) (idle ${ageS}s) — not auto-completable. Investigate, then \`cc-backlog unblock ${id}\`."
        if [ -n "$owned" ]; then
          why="wedged owned wait"
          smsg="wedged owned wait past the ${ownedmax}s ceiling — ${owned} — after ${claims} dispatch attempt(s) (claimed ${ageS}s). The worker is NOT dead and its worktree is still occupied, so this was NOT reopened: find what the wait is blocked on, then \`cc-backlog unblock ${id}\`."
        elif [ "$clrc" -eq 2 ]; then
          why="unresolvable claimer"
          smsg="claimer ${by:-<none>} could not be RESOLVED past the ${unresolvedmax}s ceiling — the liveness registry gave no answer (absent, wedged, or unparseable) and the worktree shows no live work, after ${claims} dispatch attempt(s) (claimed ${ageS}s). NOT reopened: an unanswered probe is a non-verdict, never proof of death, and reopening would fire a second worker at possibly-live work. Establish whether the worker is alive (or repair the registry), then \`cc-backlog unblock ${id}\`."
        fi
        if [ "$dry" = 1 ]; then
          printf 'cc-backlog reap: WOULD-BLOCK %s [%s]\n' "$id" "$why"
        elif cmd_transition block "$id" --needs "$smsg" --by cc-backlog-reap >/dev/null; then
          blocked=$((blocked + 1)); printf 'cc-backlog reap: BLOCK %s [%s]\n' "$id" "$why"
        fi
      elif [ "$dry" = 1 ]; then
        printf 'cc-backlog reap: WOULD-REOPEN %s [dead-worker, claimed %ss, %s attempt(s)]\n' "$id" "$ageS" "$claims"
      elif cmd_transition reopen "$id" --by cc-backlog-reap >/dev/null; then
        reopened=$((reopened + 1)); printf 'cc-backlog reap: REOPEN %s [dead-worker, claimed %ss]\n' "$id" "$ageS"
      fi
    fi
  done <<< "$analysis"

  if [ "$dry" = 1 ]; then
    printf 'cc-backlog reap (dry-run): %s non-terminal item(s) scanned — no writes\n' "$scanned"
  else
    printf 'cc-backlog reap: %s reopened, %s blocked (%s non-terminal scanned)\n' "$reopened" "$blocked" "$scanned"
  fi
}

command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "cc-backlog: jq is required" >&2; exit 1; }

case "${1:-}" in
  add)     shift; cmd_add "$@" ;;
  list)    shift; cmd_list "$@" ;;
  claim)   shift; cmd_transition claim "$@" ;;
  done)    shift; cmd_transition "done" "$@" ;;
  reopen)  shift; cmd_transition reopen "$@" ;;
  block)   shift; cmd_transition block "$@" ;;
  unblock) shift; cmd_transition unblock "$@" ;;
  reap)    shift; cmd_reap "$@" ;;
  compact) shift; cmd_compact "$@" ;;
  ""|-h|--help|help) usage ;;
  *) printf 'cc-backlog: unknown verb %s\n\n' "$1" >&2; usage >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac
